From: Hazel Smith <hazel@hazelesque.uk>
Subject: singlejar: provide off64_t typedef on musl Linux

singlejar's port.h falls through to "no off64_t typedef needed"
on Linux, expecting glibc to provide both off_t and off64_t in
<sys/types.h>.  Musl exposes only one (off_t, which IS 64-bit
when _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE is set) — so the static_assert at
the bottom of the file fails because off64_t isn't a type.

Mirror what glibc and musl actually do: on glibc define
off_t in terms of off64_t (the LFS path glibc takes when
_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64), on musl define off64_t in terms of
off_t (since musl's off_t already is 64-bit).  features.h is
included so __GLIBC__ / __GNU_LIBRARY__ resolve; either macro
catches glibc, neither catches musl.

This is the same gap p4source crosses with `-D_GNU_SOURCE
-D_LARGEFILE64_SOURCE` in CCFLAGS — see scm-infra's
copybara/.bazelrc chimera config for the bazel-side equivalent.

diff --git a/src/tools/singlejar/port.h b/src/tools/singlejar/port.h
--- a/src/tools/singlejar/port.h
+++ b/src/tools/singlejar/port.h
@@ -34,6 +34,13 @@
 typedef __int64 off64_t;
 #elif defined(__OpenBSD__)
 typedef int64_t off64_t;
+#elif defined(__GLIBC__) || defined(__linux__)
+#include <features.h>
+#if defined(__GNU_LIBRARY__) || defined(__GLIBC__)
+typedef off64_t off_t;
+#else
+typedef off_t off64_t;
+#endif
 #endif
 static_assert(sizeof(off64_t) == 8, "File offset type must be 64-bit");

